# ZFC Set Theory Axioms
# Predicates: elem(x, y) means x is an element of y
#             eq(x, y) means x equals y

# 1. Extensionality (definition of equality)
# Two sets are equal iff they have the same elements.
# This says: x = y iff x and y have the same members. Together with
# `elem_subst` below, it fully axiomatizes equality for the {eq, elem}
# signature in pure FOL (i.e., without an `eq_subst` rule built into the
# calculus).
#
# Substitution coverage. Extensionality lets us substitute into `elem`'s
# *second* argument — from eq(s, t) and elem(x, s), derive elem(x, t)
# (proof: forall_elim extensionality at (s, t), iff_elim_l on the eq, then
# iff_elim_l on the resulting forall-of-iff at the witness x). Substitution
# into `elem`'s *first* argument is NOT derivable from extensionality
# alone — that requires the next axiom.
axiom extensionality: forall x. forall y. (eq(x, y) <-> forall z. (elem(z, x) <-> elem(z, y)))

# 1b. Element-position substitution
# If x = y, then x and y have the same memberships.
#
# This is the half of equality that extensionality does not cover.
# Extensionality (axiom 1) handles the *set* position of `elem`; this
# handles the *element* position. Together with extensionality, the two
# axioms suffice to prove every concrete instance of equality
# substitution in the {eq, elem} signature, removing the need for
# `eq_subst` as a primitive proof rule.
#
# Why this can't be derived from extensionality: extensionality says
# eq(x, y) ⟺ ∀z. (elem(z, x) ↔ elem(z, y)) — it relates equality to what
# x and y *contain*, not to where x and y *are contained*. There is no
# chain in pure FOL from "x and y have the same elements" to "x and y
# belong to the same sets"; that's a separate first-order fact about how
# `elem` interacts with `eq`. Hence the extra axiom.
axiom elem_subst: forall x. forall y. forall s. (eq(x, y) -> (elem(x, s) <-> elem(y, s)))

# 2. Empty Set
# There exists a set with no elements
axiom empty_set: exists e. forall x. ~elem(x, e)

# 3. Pairing
# For any two sets a and b, there exists a set containing exactly a and b
axiom pairing: forall a. forall b. exists p. forall x. (elem(x, p) <-> (eq(x, a) | eq(x, b)))

# 4. Union
# For any set A, there exists the union of all elements of A
axiom union: forall A. exists U. forall x. (elem(x, U) <-> exists y. (elem(y, A) & elem(x, y)))

# 5. Power Set
# For any set A, there exists the set of all subsets of A
axiom power_set: forall A. exists P. forall x. (elem(x, P) <-> forall y. (elem(y, x) -> elem(y, A)))

# 6. Infinity
# There exists an infinite set (contains empty set and is closed under von Neumann successor)
# For every x in I, there exists y in I where y = x ∪ {x}
axiom infinity: exists I.
    (exists e. (elem(e, I) & forall x. ~elem(x, e))) &
    (forall x. (elem(x, I) -> exists y. (elem(y, I) &
        forall z. (elem(z, y) <-> (elem(z, x) | eq(z, x))))))

# 7. Foundation (Regularity)
# Every non-empty set has an element disjoint from it
axiom foundation: forall A. ((exists x. elem(x, A)) -> exists y. (elem(y, A) & forall z. ~(elem(z, y) & elem(z, A))))

# 8. Separation Schema (instance)
# For any set A and property P, there exists the subset of A satisfying P
# Note: This is a schema - we can only express specific instances
# Example instance for a unary predicate P:
axiom separation_P: forall A. exists B. forall x. (elem(x, B) <-> (elem(x, A) & P(x)))

# Note: Replacement and Choice axioms are in replacement_choice.fol.h,
# which depends on the function definitions in functions.fol.h.
